WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.470
Laughter, tears, and quite possibly the best
third act in any Star Wars movie, all of that's

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:08.309
what I experienced when I watched Gareth Edwards'
Rogue One!

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:12.240
Rogue One's been marketed as a standalone
story within the Star Wars universe, but while

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.241
it is tonally different from the other movies,
timing-wise it fits super-snugly before the

00:00:17.241 --> 00:00:19.550
events of the first movie in the original
trilogy.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.730
And for fans of the franchise, there are plenty
of cameos and hat tips to previous instalments.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.310
But what about the new cast?

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:30.670
Well, it's a really solid ensemble of actors,
many of whom really make you care for their

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.430
characters and give some stand-out turns in
the process.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:37.320
Unlike Daisy Ridley, who was pretty much an
unknown quantity before The Force Awakens,

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.350
you've probably already seen Felicity Jones
in movies such as The Theory of Everything.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:46.109
So, it's absolutely no surprise that, as the
rebellious Jyn Erso, Jones can play drama

00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:51.489
and tender moments to the max, but it's fantastic
to also see her so successfully pull out all

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:54.660
the stops when it comes to some fierce, badass
action.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.731
As K-2SO, a reprogrammed Imperial security
guard, Alan Tudyk gets to play a character

00:00:59.731 --> 00:01:05.310
who has no filter but a great line in backchat,
saying whatever pops into his droid head,

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:09.840
which results in a multitude of snark-filled
laugh-out-loud moments that show off Tudyk's

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.240
perfect comic timing.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.619
Donnie Yen brings a wonderful sense of calm
and composure to his blind monk character

00:01:15.619 --> 00:01:20.000
Chirrut Imwe, and Yen's incredible martial
arts and athletic abilities are also on full

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.430
display in some thrilling action scenes.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:27.079
And I loved the on-screen chemistry Yen has
with Jiang Wen, who plays his soldier friend

00:01:27.079 --> 00:01:28.079
Baze Malbus.

00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:32.520
Both actors really brought out the beautiful
bond between their two characters to great

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.520
effect.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.439
As cargo pilot Bodhi, Riz Ahmed puts in a
fine performance, and as Rebel Intelligence

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:42.100
Officer Cassian, Diego Luna gets to deliver
the hell out of a particularly stirring speech.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.439
While Ben Mendelsohn is suitably unsettling
as the deliciously villainous and ambitious

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:49.820
Director Krennic, and stands out visually
thanks to excellent work in the costume department

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:52.350
by designers David Crossman and Glyn Dillon.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:57.049
In the role of Jyn Erso's father, Galen, Mads
Mikkelsen brings out his character's strength

00:01:57.049 --> 00:01:59.710
and suffering, even during moments of exposition.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:03.299
After seeing the trailers, I've got to admit
I was a bit worried about Forest Whitaker's

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:08.720
line as outlaw rebel Saw Gerrera, 'save the
rebellion, save the dream', which every time

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.630
I saw it at the cinema, screamed Heroes' 'Save
the cheerleader, save the world' to me, and

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:14.630
not in a good way.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:18.950
So, I was glad to see that it plays out in
a much less cheesy way in the final movie,

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:22.860
and it's actually said in what I felt was
a much more plaintive tone.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.550
See, context is everything!

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:28.570
Oh, and a certain character from the previous
films returns to give you chills!

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:32.900
I really liked the way he's introduced when
we see him fully and I was fine with the amount

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.250
he appears in the film.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:38.690
With so many new characters and locations
to introduce, Rogue One does take its time

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:43.510
to build up a full head of steam and there
is some knotty plotting and certain inelegant

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:47.810
character shifts, which means it's a while
before things get going with the meat of the

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:48.810
movie.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.380
Of course, there's been a lot of talk of long
reshoots and just how much of Chris Weitz's

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.080
script was reworked by the Bourne movies'
Tony Gilroy.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.970
How many of those changes fed into some of
the film's issues and how many strengthened

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.870
it is difficult to say with absolute certainty.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:07.150
The film I'm reviewing here is obviously the
one I saw in front of me on the big screen,

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:12.370
and in this Rogue One, director Gareth Edwards
has made a bold film that's darker in tone

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:17.110
than previous Star Wars outings, that does
not shy away from the complexities and violence

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:21.800
of warfare, and that introduces conflicted
characters, many of whom worked their way

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.140
right into my affections.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:27.950
And when the film is at its best, we find
ourselves in the middle of a heist and a war

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:32.480
movie with vibes of Saving Private Ryan meets
The Dirty Dozen, via Black Hawk Down and The

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.480
Hurt Locker.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.690
In terms of spectacle, Rogue One is dazzling.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:39.731
There are so many heart-stopping and stirring
scenes in the movie that brought tears to

00:03:39.731 --> 00:03:43.230
my eyes and they build up to an all-encompassing,
incredible crescendo.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:48.290
And as the film moves into its final phase,
its editors keep niftily cutting between a

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:52.540
variety of characters and locations, ramping
up the tension.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:56.290
Cinematographer Greig Fraser, who's known
for Zero Dark Thirty, takes us right in to

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:01.090
the middle of the action, getting down and
dirty to full gritty effect, making us feel

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:06.130
every blast and explosion, as he weaves between
obstacles and enemies, just as our heroes

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:08.910
do, amid the dust, smoke and sand.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.700
During action scenes, the camera is fluid
and often handheld, and apparently director

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:17.040
Edwards regularly operated it himself, so
kudos also goes to him for the film's final

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.160
look and for also managing to give us both
amazing scale in the movie while still often

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.370
keeping action grounded on a more personal
level.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.690
Edwards and cinematographer Fraser also make
great use of the various locations where they

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:33.590
filmed, including the Maldives, for example,
they really bring out the contrast between

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:38.510
beautiful beach-side settings and the full-on
horror of war as things are blown to smithereens

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.340
beside sapphire waters.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:45.150
And perfectly matching the movie's naturalistic
cinematography is Doug Chiang and Neil Lamont's

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:49.820
production design, which brings out the realistically
rough edges of the Star Wars world.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:55.380
For some superb stunt choreography and some
truly explosive scenes, props also go to stunt

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.480
coordinator Rob Inch, who worked on The Force
Awakens and Captain Phillips, and to Oscar-winning

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:03.320
special effects supervisor Neil Corbould,
whose effects you'll have seen in Kingsman

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:04.320
and Black Hawk Down.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.220
And, of course, it couldn't be a Star Wars
movie without some spectacular visual effects,

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.060
and for those we have to thank Star Wars veteran
John Knoll, who also came up with the original

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:17.120
idea for Rogue One, as well as Mohen Leo,
who worked on Ant-Man and The Martian.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:22.160
There are some absolutely stunning moments
set out in space as well as on various planets

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:27.650
and moons, and so many of the scenes of destruction,
which we see from both god's and worm's eye

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:33.650
views, are obviously properly devastating
but also, almost perversely, things of extreme

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:34.650
beauty.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:38.770
The only place where I'd say the VFX were
slightly off was with the face of one particular

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:39.770
character.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:43.250
I think they did a good job getting 80% of
the way there in a tough situation, but at

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:48.010
first I did find it distracting, though later
I got used to it, so it didn't spoil the movie

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:49.010
for me.

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:52.940
And, of course, I've gotta give a shout-out
to BAFTA-winning Neal Scanlan and his Creature

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:57.880
Effects team for their work on the movie's
many new cool alien life forms and droids.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:02.960
As so often happens with the Star Wars movies,
some of their work flies by all too quickly,

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.610
so I can't wait to see it on DVD so I can
take a lingering look over some of their new

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:09.610
creations.

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:13.440
With his score for Rogue One, composer Michael
Giacchino has now completed the full Disney

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.110
composing package, adding Star Wars to his
scores for Disney animation Zootopia, Disney's

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:22.410
live action Tomorrowland, numerous Pixar films,
and Marvel's Doctor Strange.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.730
And what a great job he's done here too!

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:28.510
And there's even some lovely little moments
blended in at appropriate points from John

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:33.160
Williams' original Star Wars themes, which
always make me feel like a little kid again.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:37.860
To sum up: Rogue One is full of both stunning
spectacle and genuinely moving moments, and

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:42.050
introduces some engaging new characters given
life by an ensemble of accomplished actors.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:46.620
Though the film does take a while to rev up
and move through some thorny plot moments,

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:50.680
the pay-off is so worth it that it's entirely
possible that you'll leave the cinema on a

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:51.919
super-high, like I did!

00:06:51.919 --> 00:06:56.301
Rogue One is the Star Wars prequel I've been
looking for, and there's so much the film

00:06:56.301 --> 00:07:00.220
gets right that, for me, it's up there with
the very best Star Wars movies and its final

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:02.560
act is mind-blowing cinema.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.090
And I'm really looking forward to watching
it again.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:08.770
So, I'm giving Rogue One 4 and a half out
of 5 Flicks.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:10.780
Now, what did you think of Rogue One?

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:12.300
Who are your favourite new characters?

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.060
And how do you think it compares to the other
Star Wars movies?

